-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0866cd13-4086-562d-ae6a-193aa22c57ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0866cd13-4086-562d-ae6a-193aa22c57ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356501} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3a484f-84d0-5670-9864-97137baeee92" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ce94e4f-6bff-561d-bee3-cf3e011e5b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-343146" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/19d9904c5236534ee6b4f176d760268df397f644d4ea7016d1d35c4b5db6e413.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,0),$ and $(2,4).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,),\\,(\\,-2,0\\,),\\,(\\,2,4\\,).\n$$"}]}
